        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "An alkene reacts with hydrogen halide in the presence of ether to form a product, in which C1 is bonded to a hydrogen to C2 and a halogen atom."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "A reaction shows an alkene reacting with dihalide in dichloromethane to form a product, in which each carbon is bonded to a halogen group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "An alkene reacts with dihalogen in presence of water to form product and hydrogen halide. In product, C1 is bonded to hydroxyl group. C2 is bonded to halogen."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00011",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00011-alt",
              "text": "Alkene reacts with mercury (II) acetate, water in tetrahydrofuran in first step, and sodium borohydride in second step to form a product, in which C1 is bonded to hydroxyl group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "Alkene reacts with borane in tetrahydrofuran and hydrogen peroxide in hydroxide ion to form a product, in which C1 is bonded to a hydroxyl group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00013",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00013-alt",
              "text": "Alkene reacts with hydrogen in the presence of palladium on carbon  or platinum dioxide to form an alkane."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00014",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00014-alt",
              "text": "Alkene reacts with peroxyacid to form an epoxide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00015",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00015-alt",
              "text": "Alkene reacts with osmium tetroxide in the first step, sodium bisulfite water or osmium tetroxide, and N M O in the second step to form a diol."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00016",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00016-alt",
              "text": "A reaction shows an alkene reacting with trichloromethane in the presence of potassium hydroxide to form a cyclopropane, in which C 1 is bonded to two chlorine atoms."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00017",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00017-alt",
              "text": "A reaction shows an alkene reacting with diiodomethane in the presence of zinc-copper and ether to form cyclopropane."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00018",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00018-alt",
              "text": "A reaction shows an epoxide reacting with hydronium ion to form a trans-1,2-diol."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00019",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00019-alt",
              "text": "Alkene with two R groups on each carbon reacts with ozone and zinc-hydronium to form two ketones, each with a carbonyl group bonded to two R groups."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00020",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00020-alt",
              "text": "Alkene with two R groups on each carbon reacts with potassium permanganate and hydronium ion, yielding two ketones. Alkene with one R converts to carboxylic acid and carbon dioxide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00021",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00101:alt:fig-00021-alt",
              "text": "The figure shows 1,2-diol reacts with periodic acid in the presence of water to form two carbonyl compounds."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_08_EC_014.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
